{
  "label": "not_comparable",
  "factor": "other",
  "rationale": "The two selected claims address different metformin exposures. Claim A (PMID 38662670) extracts the association between the duration of metformin use and vitamin B12 deficiency (OR 1.01, 95% CI 1.00-1.02, unadjusted, directional '+'), which matches sentence 7 of its abstract. Claim B (PMID 32559822) extracts the relationship between the daily metformin dose and vitamin B12 deficiency, with the authors' explicit statement that 'No significant relationship was found between the daily metformin dose and vitamin-B12 deficiency' (sentence 10), which also matches its source. Both claims share the outcome (vitamin B12 deficiency in T2D patients on metformin) and an association-type contrast, but the exposures are not the same: length of time on metformin versus daily metformin dose. These are distinct exposure variables, and neither abstract supports collapsing one into the other (A reports dose separately from duration; B reports only dose and does not analyze duration). Although A's abstract also contains a dose finding (dose above median, adjusted OR 4.10) that could in principle contrast with B's null dose result, that is not the selected claim on side A, so the pair as extracted lacks a comparable exposure-contrast. The decisive obstacle is the exposure mismatch, a concrete source-supported difference rather than missing information.",
  "diagnostics": {
    "material_extraction_error": false,
    "error_sides": [],
    "error_fields": [],
    "uncertain_extraction": false,
    "comparison_sufficient": false
  , "source_evidence": [
    {
      "side": "a",
      "sentence_id": 7,
      "quote": "Further, the duration of diabetes, duration of metformin use, metformin dose, and hemoglobin levels were statistically associated with vitamin B12 deficiency with OR (95% CI) = 1.12 (1.03-1.19), 1.01 (1.00-1.02), 1.002 (1.001-1.002), 0.74 (0.55-0.99), respectively."
    },
    {
      "side": "b",
      "sentence_id": 10,
      "quote": "No significant relationship was found between the daily metformin dose and vitamin-B12 deficiency."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "notes": "Side A claim verified against sentence 7 (duration of metformin use, OR 1.01 (1.00-1.02), unadjusted, directional '+'); side B claim verified against sentence 10 (explicit no-relationship wording for daily metformin dose). Had side A's selected claim been its dose finding (adjusted OR 4.10 for dose above median, sentence 8), a not_replicated comparison against side B's explicit null dose result would have been plausible; as extracted, however, the exposure variables (duration vs daily dose) differ, so the pair is unfit for comparison."
} 
}
